WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.893
This is part three of mechanical ventilation: PEEP, pressures, circuits and valves.

2
00:00:06.893 --> 00:00:15.058
We start with PEEP: what it does, when to use it, how much to set, and the level the exam calls dangerous.

3
00:00:15.058 --> 00:00:20.604
Then auto-PEEP: how it forms, and how to spot it, measure it and fix it.

4
00:00:20.604 --> 00:00:30.309
Then airway pressures: what a high peak pressure means with and without a high plateau, and what low pressure alarms point to.

5
00:00:30.309 --> 00:00:34.700
We finish with ventilator circuits and exhalation valves.

6
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:48.217
PEEP increases the end-expiratory, or baseline, airway pressure: instead of returning to zero, it stays elevated, at five, eight or ten.

7
00:00:48.217 --> 00:00:50.801
It is used with another mode.

8
00:00:50.801 --> 00:00:57.394
CPAP is PEEP on a fully spontaneous patient, and EPAP is the same as PEEP.

9
00:00:57.394 --> 00:01:12.896
The chain to remember: PEEP raises the functional residual capacity, which improves ventilation-perfusion matching, which raises oxygenation and lowers the work of breathing.

10
00:01:12.896 --> 00:01:16.727
It also recruits alveoli and reduces shunt.

11
00:01:16.727 --> 00:01:22.875
That makes PEEP the number one ventilator adjustment for oxygenation.

12
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:39.119
Use PEEP for shunt or refractory hypoxemia, with a P to F ratio below two hundred; chapter eleven defines it as a PaO2 of fifty or less on an FiO2 of fifty percent or more.

13
00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:46.529
It also helps a reduced FRC or compliance, and auto-PEEP that the settings cannot fix.

14
00:01:46.529 --> 00:02:04.192
Chapter eleven's rule: below fifty percent oxygen, adjust the FiO2; at fifty percent or more, add PEEP; and as the patient improves, bring the FiO2 down to fifty percent or less first, then lower the PEEP.

15
00:02:04.192 --> 00:02:09.792
Start at five centimeters of water; the NBRC range is two to six.

16
00:02:09.792 --> 00:02:18.408
Optimal PEEP is the lowest pressure level that offers the most benefits with the least side effects.

17
00:02:18.408 --> 00:02:28.575
Chapter eleven says it keeps the saturation at eighty eight to ninety percent or more without lowering blood pressure.

18
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.685
Chapter eleven gives several ways to find it.

19
00:02:33.685 --> 00:02:41.875
In volume control, choose the PEEP with the highest static compliance, and ignore the peak pressure.

20
00:02:41.875 --> 00:02:52.030
In pressure control, choose the PEEP that gives the largest increase in exhaled tidal volume at a constant driving pressure.

21
00:02:52.030 --> 00:03:06.198
On a pressure-volume curve, PEEP is typically two to three centimeters of water above the lower inflection point; the RCP 105 deck says at the lower inflection point itself.

22
00:03:06.198 --> 00:03:12.586
After a recruitment maneuver, use the PEEP with the best compliance, plus two.

23
00:03:12.586 --> 00:03:19.137
Or set it just before the carbon dioxide fraction of each breath begins to fall.

24
00:03:19.137 --> 00:03:32.650
In ARDS, the Procedures deck pairs thirty percent oxygen with a PEEP of about five, sixty percent with about ten, and one hundred percent with twenty to twenty four.

25
00:03:34.067 --> 00:03:56.935
The exam answer for the dangerous level comes from a starred RCP 202 slide: a PEEP above ten centimeters of water, a mean airway pressure above thirty, or a peak pressure above fifty is associated with an increased incidence of alveolar rupture, or barotrauma.

26
00:03:56.935 --> 00:04:00.981
Chang's safe list agrees, with PEEP below ten.

27
00:04:00.981 --> 00:04:11.975
And a PEEP above fifteen with an intra-abdominal pressure above twenty harms the cardiovascular, renal and pulmonary systems.

28
00:04:11.975 --> 00:04:13.734
There is a conflict.

29
00:04:13.734 --> 00:04:31.853
The Procedures notes call PEEP below ten older practice, since many ARDS patients need ten to fifteen or more, and Hess limits PEEP when the plateau is above thirty or the driving pressure is above fifteen.

30
00:04:31.853 --> 00:04:34.492
On the exam, answer above ten.

31
00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:44.434
The major downside of PEEP is lower venous return, which lowers cardiac output and causes hypotension.

32
00:04:44.434 --> 00:04:49.204
Barotrauma, such as a pneumothorax, is the other big one.

33
00:04:49.204 --> 00:05:02.089
PEEP can also raise intracranial pressure by impeding cerebral venous drainage; the Operating Modes slide says this applies in patients with normal lungs.

34
00:05:02.089 --> 00:05:17.150
It raises pulmonary artery and central venous pressures while lowering aortic pressure, it reduces hepatic perfusion, and excess PEEP compresses capillaries, which adds dead space.

35
00:05:18.567 --> 00:05:19.914
Pause and think.

36
00:05:19.914 --> 00:05:28.416
A patient's PEEP is raised from ten to fourteen to improve oxygenation, and the blood pressure falls.

37
00:05:28.416 --> 00:05:35.067
What is the likely mechanism, and is fourteen above the exam's dangerous level?

38
00:05:35.967 --> 00:05:41.967
[6-second practice pause]

39
00:05:42.467 --> 00:05:52.507
PEEP lowers venous return, which lowers cardiac output and causes hypotension; that is the major downside of PEEP.

40
00:05:52.507 --> 00:06:02.019
And yes, on the exam fourteen is above the dangerous level of ten, which is associated with more barotrauma.

41
00:06:02.019 --> 00:06:11.267
In practice many ARDS patients need ten to fifteen, but optimal PEEP should not lower the blood pressure.

42
00:06:12.667 --> 00:06:23.956
Auto-PEEP is unintentional PEEP from incomplete exhalation, also called intrinsic, inadvertent or occult PEEP, or dynamic hyperinflation.

43
00:06:23.956 --> 00:06:31.949
The manometer may read zero while the distal pressure is as high as fifteen centimeters of water.

44
00:06:31.949 --> 00:06:48.264
Causes are a high rate, airway obstruction, insufficient inspiratory flow, an I to E ratio near one to one or inverse, excessive pressure support, IMV breath stacking, and inverse ratio ventilation.

45
00:06:48.264 --> 00:06:56.422
The highest risk is high airway resistance, high compliance, or both, with a short expiratory time.

46
00:06:56.422 --> 00:07:05.321
Effects are more work of breathing, harder triggering, hypotension from lower venous return, and barotrauma.

47
00:07:05.321 --> 00:07:13.067
In volume control it raises the peak pressure; in pressure control it lowers the tidal volume.

48
00:07:14.467 --> 00:07:26.047
To detect it, look for expiratory flow that does not return to zero before the next breath; chapter eleven says this is likely to appear on the NBRC.

49
00:07:26.047 --> 00:07:32.731
Other clues are a patient rate higher than the ventilator rate, and a high PEEP alarm.

50
00:07:32.731 --> 00:07:47.110
To measure it, do an end-expiratory pause in a relaxed, sedated or paralyzed patient, and subtract the set PEEP from the pause pressure: twelve minus five is seven centimeters of water.

51
00:07:47.110 --> 00:08:02.577
To fix it, give more time to exhale: lower the rate, raise the inspiratory flow in volume control, lower the tidal volume, shorten the inspiratory time in pressure control, and give a bronchodilator.

52
00:08:02.577 --> 00:08:16.567
Then, only for spontaneously breathing patients, add applied PEEP up to eighty to ninety percent of the auto-PEEP, per chapter three; Hess says seventy five to eighty five percent.

53
00:08:17.967 --> 00:08:19.854
Pause and work it out.

54
00:08:19.854 --> 00:08:28.688
On volume control at a rate of twenty, an end-expiratory pause reads fifteen, and the set PEEP is five.

55
00:08:28.688 --> 00:08:34.692
How much auto-PEEP is there, and what setting changes would reduce it?

56
00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:41.600
[6-second practice pause]

57
00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:47.211
Fifteen minus five is ten centimeters of water of auto-PEEP.

58
00:08:47.211 --> 00:08:49.936
The goal is more time to exhale.

59
00:08:49.936 --> 00:09:02.116
On volume control, lower the rate, raise the inspiratory flow so inspiration ends sooner, or lower the tidal volume, and give a bronchodilator.

60
00:09:02.116 --> 00:09:08.675
Applied PEEP comes after that, and only for spontaneously breathing patients.

61
00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:15.060
The peak pressure overcomes airway resistance plus compliance.

62
00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:23.220
The plateau pressure, measured during an end-inspiratory pause with no flow, reflects compliance only.

63
00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:31.780
The deck's rule: when peak and plateau move together, think lungs; when only the peak moves, think airways.

64
00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:46.980
A higher peak with an unchanged plateau means higher resistance: secretions, a mucus plug, a kinked or bitten tube, bronchospasm, airway edema, water in the tubing, or a higher flow setting.

65
00:09:46.980 --> 00:10:01.300
A higher peak and a higher plateau mean lower compliance: ARDS, pulmonary edema, pneumonia, atelectasis, a tension pneumothorax, pleural effusion, mainstem intubation, or ascites.

66
00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:23.706
Chapter eleven, following the AARC, sets the high-pressure alarm at thirty five centimeters of water at first, if the peak pressure is not yet measured, then ten above the observed peak once the patient is stable; RCP 105 says ten to fifteen above.

67
00:10:23.706 --> 00:10:32.176
Causes include water in the circuit, secretions, a kinked tube, a tension pneumothorax and coughing.

68
00:10:32.176 --> 00:10:37.428
Reaching the limit ends the breath, which is pressure cycling.

69
00:10:37.428 --> 00:10:51.065
A circuit obstruction gives low-volume and high-pressure alarms, and an expiratory obstruction is the more serious, causing rebreathing, asphyxia and barotrauma.

70
00:10:51.065 --> 00:11:00.975
The ceilings: keep the peak below fifty and the plateau below thirty; Chang prints below thirty five, but use thirty.

71
00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:13.433
The low inspiratory pressure alarm sits ten centimeters of water below the observed peak, or below the set pressure in pressure control.

72
00:11:13.433 --> 00:11:25.763
Paired with the low tidal volume alarm, which chapter eleven sets one hundred fifty milliliters from the set volume, it detects leaks and disconnection.

73
00:11:25.763 --> 00:11:31.036
Hess calls the patient-disconnect alarm the most important alarm.

74
00:11:31.036 --> 00:11:48.153
Causes are circuit disconnection; circuit leaks, among the most common causes of lost volume and pressure; a cuff leak, which Hess hears as an end-inspiratory squeak over the trachea; and ventilator malfunction.

75
00:11:48.153 --> 00:11:52.533
A low PEEP alarm usually means a circuit or cuff leak.

76
00:11:52.533 --> 00:12:04.702
On the graphics, a volume-time curve that does not return to baseline means a leak, often at the cuff; chapter eleven says this is likely on the NBRC.

77
00:12:04.702 --> 00:12:10.624
Improving compliance or resistance also lowers the peak, which is benign.

78
00:12:10.624 --> 00:12:17.600
The rule: alarm, look at the patient, then the ventilator, find the cause, and fix it.

79
00:12:19.033 --> 00:12:20.542
Pause and read it.

80
00:12:20.542 --> 00:12:29.256
A ventilated patient's peak pressure climbs from twenty eight to forty, but the plateau stays at twenty.

81
00:12:29.256 --> 00:12:32.608
Airways or lungs, and what do you check?

82
00:12:33.533 --> 00:12:39.533
[6-second practice pause]

83
00:12:40.033 --> 00:12:45.513
Only the peak moved, so think airways: airway resistance has risen.

84
00:12:45.513 --> 00:12:57.045
Look at the patient, then check for secretions or a mucus plug, a kinked or bitten tube, bronchospasm, airway edema, and water in the tubing.

85
00:12:57.045 --> 00:13:05.633
If the plateau had risen too, you would think lungs, such as ARDS, a pneumothorax or mainstem intubation.

86
00:13:07.033 --> 00:13:09.475
There are three circuit types.

87
00:13:09.475 --> 00:13:23.392
A single-limb circuit with a valve puts the exhalation valve near the patient; a pneumatic signal closes it for inspiration, and its residual pressure often sets the PEEP.

88
00:13:23.392 --> 00:13:26.485
It is used in home care and transport.

89
00:13:26.485 --> 00:13:41.867
A double-limb, or wye, circuit keeps the exhalation valve inside the ventilator: the inspiratory limb runs to the fifteen millimeter connector, and the expiratory limb returns to the valve.

90
00:13:41.867 --> 00:13:43.658
It is the ICU circuit.

91
00:13:43.658 --> 00:13:56.029
A single-limb circuit with no valve uses a leak port, a known leak that the ventilator calculates and subtracts; it is used for noninvasive ventilation.

92
00:13:56.029 --> 00:14:06.283
Adult tubing is large-bore and corrugated; neonatal tubing is small and low-compliance, so less volume is lost to compression.

93
00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:18.626
Mechanical dead space in a dual-limb circuit runs from the wye to the airway, so anything added there, such as an HME, adds dead space.

94
00:14:18.626 --> 00:14:29.310
The circuit itself is compressible, as much as three to five milliliters per centimeter of water, according to Hess, so keep it low.

95
00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:35.542
Corrected tidal volume equals the expired volume minus the compressed volume.

96
00:14:35.542 --> 00:14:46.711
For example, three milliliters per centimeter of water, times a pressure change of sixty minus ten, is one hundred fifty milliliters lost.

97
00:14:46.711 --> 00:14:50.354
This matters most in neonates and pediatrics.

98
00:14:50.354 --> 00:15:02.170
Do not change circuits routinely, nor the attached humidifiers, HMEs or closed-suction systems; change them when visibly soiled or malfunctioning.

99
00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:09.050
If you suspect a major problem, bag the patient with a resuscitator and a PEEP valve.

100
00:15:10.467 --> 00:15:13.401
The exhalation valve has two jobs.

101
00:15:13.401 --> 00:15:25.224
First, it separates the phases: the expiratory valve is closed during inspiration, and the inspiratory valve is closed during expiration.

102
00:15:25.224 --> 00:15:36.443
ICU ventilators have an internal electromechanical expiratory valve; single-limb circuits use a pneumatic line to close the valve.

103
00:15:36.443 --> 00:15:43.778
Second, it sets PEEP: the expiratory valve controls positive end-expiratory pressure.

104
00:15:43.778 --> 00:15:56.637
An active exhalation valve lets the patient breathe during a mandatory pressure breath, which is how APRV works, and vents cough pressure in VC plus.

105
00:15:56.637 --> 00:16:03.972
An emergency expiratory valve gives a second exit if the expiratory valve is blocked.

106
00:16:03.972 --> 00:16:15.536
Expiratory and PEEP valve problems are common, and an expiratory obstruction is the more serious, worst on a single-limb NPPV circuit.

107
00:16:15.536 --> 00:16:26.842
Ribavirin can crystallize and occlude expiratory valves, and an expiratory HEPA filter must be heated to avoid condensate blockage.

108
00:16:28.267 --> 00:16:30.742
Here is part three in brief.

109
00:16:30.742 --> 00:16:44.530
PEEP raises the baseline, increases FRC and oxygenation, and is the first ventilator fix for oxygenation once the FiO2 reaches fifty percent; start at five.

110
00:16:44.530 --> 00:16:54.872
On the exam, a PEEP above ten is the dangerous level, and its major downside is hypotension from lower venous return.

111
00:16:54.872 --> 00:17:05.214
Auto-PEEP comes from incomplete exhalation; measure it with an expiratory pause, and fix it with more time to exhale.

112
00:17:05.214 --> 00:17:13.169
When peak and plateau rise together, think lungs; when only the peak rises, think airways.

113
00:17:13.169 --> 00:17:16.881
Low pressures mean leaks or disconnection.

114
00:17:16.881 --> 00:17:25.367
Part four covers compliance, humidity, cuffs, the equation of motion, I to E ratios and ARDSNet.
